#### `BPlusTree::rangeQuery` in `33-buzzdb.cpp:621-647`

Demonstrates an efficient range query. The code first traverses the tree to find the starting leaf, then scans horizontally using the 'next' pointers (the sequence set).

```cpp
621 std::vector<Value> rangeQuery(const Key& lowerBound, const Key& upperBound) const {
 622 std::vector<Value> result;
 623 auto node = root;
 624	
 625 // Traverse to the leaf node containing the lowerBound
 626 while (node && !node->isLeaf) {
 627 size_t i = 0;
 628 while (i < node->keys.size() && lowerBound > node->keys[i]) {
 629 ++i;
 630 }
 631 node = node->children[i];
 632 }
 633	
 634 // Perform leaf-level traversal using `next`
 635 while (node) {
 636 for (size_t i = 0; i < node->keys.size(); ++i) {
 637 if (node->keys[i] > upperBound) return result;
 638 if (node->keys[i] >= lowerBound) {
 639 std::cout << node->values[i] << " ";
 640 result.push_back(node->values[i]);
 641 }
 642 }
 643 node = node->next;
 644 }
 645	
 646 return result;
 647 }
```

This horizontal scan is incredibly fast. On a disk-based system, the database can often pre-fetch the next few leaf nodes, anticipating that the scan will continue. The I/O pattern becomes largely sequential, which is the most efficient way to read from any storage device. We have completely avoided the expensive process of repeatedly traversing the tree from the root for each key in the range.

A full table scan can be seen as a special case of a range query where the range covers all possible keys. The experimental `z-btree.cpp` file contains a clean example of traversing the entire sequence set to print all keys in sorted order:

```cpp
// Traverse down to the first leaf
while (node && !node->isLeaf) {
 node = node->children.front();
}

// Now traverse through the leaf nodes using the next pointer
std::cout << "Traversing leaf nodes: ";
while (node != nullptr) {
 for (const auto& key : node->keys) {
 std::cout << key << " ";
 }
 node = node->next;
}
```

