#### 4.4.3.2 Flushing a Page

Flushing a page is the inverse of loading: it takes an in-memory page and writes its contents back to the corresponding location on disk. This is how changes are made permanent.

```cpp
// Write a page to disk
void flush(uint16_t page_id) {
 size_t page_offset = page_id * PAGE_SIZE; 

 // Move the write pointer
 fileStream.seekp(page_offset, std::ios::beg);
 fileStream.write(pages[page_id]->page_data.get(), PAGE_SIZE); 
 fileStream.flush();
}
```

### 2.2.1 The `Field`: A Self-Describing Value

At the heart of BuzzDB's flexible data model is the `Field` class. It is the atomic unit of data, the smallest piece of information the database can handle. Think of it as a smart container. Unlike a raw `int` or `std::string`, which only holds a value, a `Field` holds both a value and a description of that value. It is self-contained and self-describing. This design is the foundation for building a system that can manage data without having its schema baked into the source code.

Let's examine the structure of the `Field` class to see how it achieves this.

```cpp
class Field {
public:
 FieldType type;

 std::unique_ptr<char[]> data;
 size_t data_length;

 // ... constructors and methods ...
};
```

This classic design has a clever property: the page is full only when the slot array and the record data "meet in the middle." This ensures that all available space can be used, regardless of whether the pressure comes from adding many small records (growing the slot array) or a few very large ones (consuming the data area).

Let's see how BuzzDB implements this fundamental structure. In version v014, the simple `std::vector`-based `Page` is replaced with a `SlottedPage` class. This class manages a raw byte buffer, `page_data`, which is our fixed-size block of memory.

```cpp
class SlottedPage {
public:
 std::unique_ptr<char[]> page_data = std::make_unique<char[]>(PAGE_SIZE);
 // ...
};
```

### 3.2.1 From a Vector of Tuples to a Page

In its earliest, purely in-memory incarnation, BuzzDB managed its data in the most straightforward way possible: a standard C++ vector. Each record, or `Tuple`, was stored in a `std::vector`, a flexible, dynamically-sized array that lives entirely in main memory.

```cpp
// From 07-buzzdb.cpp
class BuzzDB {
private:
 // a vector of Tuple unique pointers acting as a table
 std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Tuple>> table;
 // ...
};
```

The call to `tuples.erase()` would trigger a ripple effect. If we deleted the first of 100 tuples, the remaining 99 would have to be shifted one position to the left to fill the gap. On disk, this translates to reading and rewriting a potentially large amount of data, turning a conceptually simple operation into a performance bottleneck.

The slotted page architecture solves this problem with remarkable efficiency. Let's look at the implementation in `SlottedPage::deleteTuple` from v014:

```cpp
// From 14-buzzdb.cpp (the new way)
void deleteTuple(size_t index) {
 Slot* slot_array = reinterpret_cast<Slot*>(page_data.get());
 size_t slot_itr = 0;
 for (; slot_itr < MAX_SLOTS; slot_itr++) {
 if(slot_itr == index and
 slot_array[slot_itr].empty == false){
 slot_array[slot_itr].empty = true;
 break;
 }
 }
}
```

### 7.4.2 Unclustered Indexes: A Separate Logical Order

In contrast, an **unclustered index** (often called a secondary index) is a data structure that is separate from the data file. Its logical order is independent of the physical order of the data rows. The data itself is typically stored in a structure called a **heap**, which is an unordered collection of records. New rows are simply added wherever there is available space.

The unclustered index contains the index key values, and for each key, a "pointer" to the corresponding data record. This pointer, often called a Record ID (RID), uniquely identifies the location of the row, typically by encoding its page number and the slot number within that page. Because unclustered indexes are separate structures, a single table can have **many** of them. You could have one unclustered index on `LastName`, another on `Department`, and a third on `StartDate`, all pointing to the same underlying data heap.

This is precisely the model that BuzzDB employs. The `HashIndex` is a structure held in memory, completely separate from the on-disk data file managed by the `StorageManager`. The process of building this index, shown in the `scanTableToBuildIndex` method, makes this separation clear.

```cpp
